meta {
  name: Approve Blocked
  type: http
  seq: 12
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/deal-audits/deals/{{auditDealId}}/review
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{auditReviewerToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "decision": "approve",
    "notes": "Looks fine."
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 409 Conflict", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(409);
  });

  test("says which items still need a pass", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.code).to.equal("audit_items_not_passed");
    expect(body.unmet).to.be.an("array").and.not.be.empty;
    expect(body.unmet[0]).to.have.property("id");
    expect(body.unmet[0]).to.have.property("itemName");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /deal-audits/deals/:dealId/review — approve refused (PAC-106)

  **Approve needs every item passed.** The manager rules item by item on the
  Deal Audit page; an audit with any item unruled or failed cannot be approved.
  The rule was settled 19 Oct 2026: an item that does not apply is passed with
  a note, never skipped.

  Runs right after the producer submits, before any verdict — so nothing has
  been passed and the approval is refused with the list of what is left. On a
  re-run of the collection the earlier round's passes stand (a resubmit keeps
  them) but the failed item was cleared, so there is still something unmet.

  ## Response `409`
  ```json
  {
    "statusCode": 409,
    "error": "Conflict",
    "code": "audit_items_not_passed",
    "message": "Every item must pass before the audit can be approved.",
    "unmet": [{ "id": "665f...ac1", "itemName": "Good Student" }]
  }
  ```

  The page disables Approve from the same rule (`audit.canApprove`,
  `audit.unmet` on **Get Deal Audit Review**), so a user only meets this 409
  through a stale tab.
}
